1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,327
A song asks what the world will be like in
a hundred years if having children goes

2
00:00:11,327 --> 00:00:12,850
out of fashion.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,923
Remember that famous song by the
Belarusian group Verasy? It played

4
00:00:16,923 --> 00:00:19,850
everywhere in the Soviet Union in the
early 1980s.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:29,850
Only 40 years have passed, not a hundred,
but the results are already quite clear.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,831
There is one difference: the reasons for
today's low birth rate in Russia differ

7
00:00:34,831 --> 00:00:37,850
fundamentally from those in the late
Soviet Union.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,750
Today, economic factors come to the fore.
I want to discuss this with our guest,

9
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,850
Robert Iskanderovich Nigmatulin.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,225
Robert Iskanderovich Nigmatulin was born
in Moscow on June 17, 1940. He is a Soviet

11
00:00:55,225 --> 00:00:57,850
and Russian scientist and educator.

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,408
Since 2017 he has been scientific director
of the Russian Academy of Sciences'

13
00:01:01,408 --> 00:01:02,850
Shirshov Institute of Oceanology.

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,567
He is an academician of the Russian
Academy of Sciences, former president of

15
00:01:07,567 --> 00:01:09,850
the Bashkortostan Academy of Sciences,

16
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,850
a former member of the third State Duma
and a recipient of the USSR State Prize.

17
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:25,413
Given the situation in our country, do you
think there is a unique solution to the

18
00:01:25,413 --> 00:01:30,850
demographic problem we face, which seems
set to grow?

19
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:40,875
Well, the demographic problem is a sharp
fall in the birth rate. The first reason

20
00:01:40,875 --> 00:01:41,850
is this:

21
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:49,155
the women giving birth now were born in
the 1990s. Those who were never born

22
00:01:49,155 --> 00:01:50,850
cannot give birth.

23
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,781
The blow society suffered in the 1990s is
making itself felt now. Those women who

24
00:01:56,781 --> 00:01:59,850
would be having children today aren't
here.

25
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:09,381
That's the first reason. Since 2015, the
birth rate has fallen by 40%. Forty

26
00:02:09,381 --> 00:02:11,850
percent, since 2015.

27
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,441
The second reason is that most of our
population lives in great poverty. By

28
00:02:17,441 --> 00:02:20,850
European standards, 75% of our people are
poor.

29
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,397
Some are extremely poor. They say around
20 million are living in particularly

30
00:02:26,397 --> 00:02:27,850
difficult conditions.

31
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:36,286
A family cannot afford a second child,
even though at least a second child is

32
00:02:36,286 --> 00:02:42,850
needed. This is the second reason for the
falling birth rate.

33
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:54,850
We should compare our minimum wage with
Europe, not Africa, shouldn't we?

34
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,631
On that comparison, it should be worth
roughly 1,000 liters of gasoline. Our

35
00:02:59,631 --> 00:03:00,850
minimum wage is low.

36
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:09,315
Most people earn very little. Working men
earn low wages. That's the second reason

37
00:03:09,315 --> 00:03:11,850
so few children are born.

38
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:19,812
Despite revolutionary advances in
medicine, 150,000 more people die here

39
00:03:19,812 --> 00:03:22,850
than under Soviet rule. Why?

40
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:31,850
Medical care is inaccessible. Let's look
at the 1990s and 2000s.

41
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,412
In the 1990s the birth rate fell
dramatically and mortality rose

42
00:03:37,412 --> 00:03:38,850
catastrophically.

43
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:48,081
After Putin came to power, around 2003,
healthcare spending began to rise and

44
00:03:48,081 --> 00:03:49,850
mortality fell.

45
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:57,737
It fell from 16.5 to 13.2 per thousand.
Under Soviet rule it was 10–11; Europe was

46
00:03:57,737 --> 00:04:00,850
slightly lower, roughly the same.

47
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,850
Europe gradually brought it down to nine.
Here, as you can see, it stopped falling.

48
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:15,197
Before COVID, 1.8 million people died:
200,000 more than under Soviet rule.

49
00:04:15,197 --> 00:04:16,850
That's before COVID.

50
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,824
During COVID our country's leaders,
including Putin, said we had come through

51
00:04:22,824 --> 00:04:25,850
it successfully. How was that a success?

52
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:34,138
In two years, the death rate relative to
population was twice that of America and

53
00:04:34,138 --> 00:04:36,850
three times that of Europe.

54
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,968
Although healthcare funding increased
after Putin came to power, later in the

55
00:04:43,968 --> 00:04:49,850
2000s people began cutting hospital beds
and rural medical posts.

56
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:57,314
— Optimization, yes. — Healthcare became
inaccessible to many people. Moscow is

57
00:04:57,314 --> 00:05:01,850
better off; the regions are in a very poor
state.

58
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:09,585
I have acquaintances in England who say
health standards there have fallen too,

59
00:05:09,585 --> 00:05:12,850
but the death rate is still lower.

60
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,850
So the causes are socioeconomic, along
with harmful reforms.

61
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,036
Public healthcare funding as a share of
GDP is half the European level. Many

62
00:05:27,036 --> 00:05:31,850
people there complain already; ours is
twice as low.

63
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:38,019
Robert Iskanderovich, let me ask another
question. Social security in Western

64
00:05:38,019 --> 00:05:41,850
Europe is undoubtedly quite strong and is
funded.

65
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,493
Yet those countries also face a falling
birth rate, for reasons that are

66
00:05:47,493 --> 00:05:50,850
ideological rather than economic, let's
say.

67
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:59,037
We came from villages. My grandparents had
six or eight children; in my father's

68
00:05:59,037 --> 00:06:01,850
generation there were three.

69
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:08,886
Education takes time. I want my daughter
to receive a proper education, too. So

70
00:06:08,886 --> 00:06:11,850
women start having children later.

71
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:18,088
About two children would still be enough
to aim for, although the math says an

72
00:06:18,088 --> 00:06:22,850
average of 2.2 children is needed. Let's
at least reach that.

73
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:30,375
Is it hopeless? No. Supporting motherhood
is a very serious issue. More children

74
00:06:30,375 --> 00:06:31,850
need to be born.

75
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:39,412
What does that require? Protecting
mothers, especially when raising a second

76
00:06:39,412 --> 00:06:41,850
child, let alone a third.

77
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,950
She should receive a kind of salary. Call
it an allowance, a salary, a stipend,

78
00:06:45,950 --> 00:06:46,850
whatever you like,

79
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:53,515
but it should help her support the family
until the child is at least five to seven.

80
00:06:53,515 --> 00:06:56,850
This is an issue of the highest
importance.

81
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,850
Where would the money come from?
Progressive taxation and the state budget.

82
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,142
I won't overcomplicate it. It seems to me
that solving the country's demographic

83
00:07:06,142 --> 00:07:07,850
problem is entirely in our hands.

84
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,466
Some have already made their contribution,
so to speak; some are in the process, and

85
00:07:13,466 --> 00:07:16,850
others have yet to take part in this
difficult task.

86
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:23,538
I invite you all to share your suggestions
in the comments and, if I may say so, your

87
00:07:23,538 --> 00:07:27,000
experience. Your comments are always
welcome.

